    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d140">

                <pb facs="0286" n="162" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg162"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">813.00/1157: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Salvador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Schuyler</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >San Salvador</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-12-16T13:00:00-06:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 16,
                                1921—1 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received December 17—10:40
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>68. I saw the President this morning and left with him a memorandum embodying
                    your December 14, 5 p.m.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d140-f1"/> He was greatly <pb
                        facs="0287" n="163" xml:id="pg163"/> pleased and will publish it locally to
                    strengthen position he has taken, stating that under no circumstances would he
                    interfere by force in Guatemala or to impose a federation.</p>
                <p>He states that policy of non-interference is approved by all elements of
                    Salvadorean people except so-called Unionists in Santa Ana and he hopes your
                    declaration [will] quiet latter for the time at least. Government has situation
                    well in hand.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schuyler</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d140-f1" n="51" xml:id="d140-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; see telegram no. 43, Dec. 14, to the Chargé in Nicaragua, <ref
                        target="#pg161">p. 161</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
